From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dalton Alexandre <alley.nightly_0b@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:49:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: Add the T8010 Always-On Processor driver

The AOP of the A10 runs RTKit firmware for the motion sensors and the
always-on microphones. Unlike on later SoCs, the bootloader does not
load it, so this driver does:

- the main image goes into the AOP's SRAM and the external segment into
  coherent memory. The boot argument block, located through the image
  header, gets fresh random PRNG and NONC values, the ECID that the
  bootloader put into the AOP's node (removed from the tree once read),
  and the external segment's address (EC0p);
- the AOP is started through its CPU control and boot control registers
  and booted with mainline RTKit over the A7 IOP mailbox, sending the
  initial power state as m1n1 does for these coprocessors.

Its services talk AFK/EPIC over shared rings on the application
endpoints, as on later SoCs (the Asahi Linux AOP driver). Each service
announced by the firmware gets a child platform device if the AOP's node
has a child of the same name. The child's driver receives the service's
reports through apple_aop_listen() and sends requests with
apple_aop_call(). Reports nobody listens to are dumped as debug output.

The firmware cannot be restarted once stopped, so the AOP starts once
per boot: the driver cannot be unbound and keeps its module loaded, and
it halts the AOP at shutdown.

[Orchard: from dl-alexandre/orchard db7f4e7 and dl-alexandre/omarchy-a
 6734dd5 (aop.c, firmware.py; orchard PR #36, omarchy-a PR #23): the
 tested boot sequence, firmware patching, AFK handshake and ring
 handling, moved from the userspace service and its DMA owner into the
 kernel on mainline RTKit; the bootargs block is found through the image
 header instead of fixed offsets.]
Signed-off-by: Dalton Alexandre <alley.nightly_0b@icloud.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
